# Meet CPU preview latency and memory budgets

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical report primitives are not measurements. Finite acceptance below
permits theory-only evidence without claiming a measured performance result.

Benchmark long pages, many images, dense equations, zoom, rapid edits, and final
export on ordinary integrated-graphics computers with no compute GPU.

Current executable evidence now records transport-neutral CPU benchmark
observations for those six workload scenarios. Each observation is explicitly
Preview or Final, retains caller-owned latency and peak-memory measurements, and
names a caller-owned machine/configuration classified as having no discrete GPU.
A completeness validator requires evidence for all six scenarios and requires
both Preview and Final quality to be represented without demanding a Cartesian
scenario-by-quality matrix. Successful admission now seals the exact caller
observation sequence for later budget policy without interpreting its values.

A leave-one-scenario-out matrix proves each workload is independently required
before quality-role completeness can pass. The 64-case complete-scenario quality
oracle is complemented by an exhaustive 256-state corpus crossing every
scenario-presence mask with absent, Preview-only, Final-only, or both quality
roles. It pins scenario omissions before quality-role omissions through direct
and sealed admission. Numeric budgets, units, representative machine tiers,
benchmark execution, render scheduling, and pass/fail policy remain open.

## Finite CPU-only performance envelope and workload matrix

**Inputs:** an explicitly recorded benchmark machine configuration,
accepted ten-page A4 notebook fixture, deterministic Preview/Final profile,
six representative workload scenarios and measured process latency/peak
resident memory. **Outputs:** an inspectable performance report with each
scenario/quality and a bounded pass/fail verdict, or `TheoryOnly` when the
renderer or machine measurements are unavailable. No GPU compute is required.

**One bounded target machine:** x86-64 quad-core CPU, 16 GiB system RAM,
integrated graphics only, no GPU compute path, local SSD and a declared
browser/OS/render-engine version. The accepted `Preview` resolution is 96
DPI; `Final` is 300 DPI as in P5-004. Use one deterministic seed, fixed
page bounds, 256 x 256 CPU tiles, 256 MiB per-page working allocation cap
and at most 512 MiB peak resident memory for the renderer process. These
values are a proposed reference configuration, not benchmark measurements.

**Six finite workloads and proposed targets:**

| Workload | Reproducible fixture | Budget |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Long pages | 10 A4 pages, 20 text blocks each | Preview p95 250 ms |
| Many images | 8 images, 2048 x 2048 pixels | Preview p95 500 ms |
| Dense equations | 40 equations on 1 page | Preview p95 350 ms |
| Zoom | 60%, 100%, 160% on page 1 | Preview p95 250 ms |
| Rapid edits | 10 sequential text edits | Preview p95 300 ms |
| Final export | Same 10-page document | Final at most 60 s |

**Measurement rule:** collect at least 20 repeated completed Preview events
per Preview scenario after one declared warm-up and one complete Final Export
for the ten-page fixture. Record monotonic elapsed time, p95 where defined,
peak RSS, output identity, measured cache state, CPU model, OS and run count.
Quality modes share vectors/seeds/materials/physical geometry. The benchmark
is only valid if generated results are complete and the renderer neither
changes content nor invokes a compute GPU to meet the budgets.

**Worked example:** an illustrative 10-page fixture yields a measured p95 of
210 ms in the long-page Preview scenario, 470 ms for the image-heavy page,
and 48 s for Final Export at 420 MiB renderer RSS. These three hypothetical
figures satisfy their proposed numerical targets, but the other scenarios
are unmeasured; the overall result is `Incomplete`, not `Passed`. If the
renderer is unavailable, report all six designs as `TheoryOnly` and do not
claim interactive speed.

**Refusal example:** process RSS reaches 600 MiB, Preview silently lowers
DPI, PDF pages are missing, a GPU acceleration path is used, or one scenario
is absent. The result does not pass; it records exact failing configuration,
quality mode, workload and accepted page identity. No unbounded retry loop
or hidden cache eviction is acceptable as a substitute for measurement.

**Assumptions and limits:** current domains can store all six workload
families, Preview/Final roles, machine descriptors and observed latency/RSS
without inventing numbers. They do not perform rasterization, benchmarks,
measurement, profile cost enforcement or resource scheduling. This finite
proposal sets one realistic target without claiming hardware performance.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for benchmark evidence structure; theoretical for
  budgets, actual CPU rendering and measurement, 2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** fixed integrated-graphics CPU target, six workload fixtures,
  latency/memory bounds, incomplete-case example and failure handling.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** benchmark harness, tested p95,
  enforced memory cap, renderer latency or Final PDF timing.
